---
import ComponentDoc from '../../layouts/ComponentDoc.astro';
import Section from '@esa/docs/Section.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/ApiTable.astro';

const describe = {
  'lng-lat': 'Where the marker sits, as <code>[lng, lat]</code>. Longitude first &mdash; the GeoJSON order, not Leaflet&rsquo;s.',
  draggable: 'Let the user move the pin. Emits <code>markermove</code> when they let go.',
  selected: 'Grows the default pin. Reflected, so page CSS can react with an <code>esa-map-marker[selected]</code> rule.',
  anchor: 'Which part of the marker sits on the coordinate.',
};

const events = [
  {
    name: 'markermove',
    type: '{ lngLat: [number, number] }',
    description:
      'The user finished dragging. <code>lngLat</code> is plain numbers, and the property is updated to match, so reading it back is truthful.',
  },
];
---
<ComponentDoc name="Map Marker" category="Maps" slug="esa-map-marker" type="wc" status="stable"
  summary={'A DOM marker inside <a href="/components/esa-map">esa-map</a> that holds whatever you slot into it &mdash; a badge, an avatar, a status pill &mdash; and can be dragged. Renders a default pin when given no content.'}>

  <Section id="preview" title="Preview">
    <p>
      Live examples are on the <a href="/components/esa-map#markers">Map page</a>: labelled cities
      with popups, and a draggable pin with a coordinate readout.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="ceiling" title="DOM marker or GL layer — a capability, not a performance setting">
    <p>
      This is the marker that can hold arbitrary HTML and be dragged. A GL point layer draws
      circles, sprite icons and text, nothing else, and cannot be dragged at all. The trade is a
      ceiling:
    </p>
    <div class="api-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>DOM marker (this)</th><th>GL point layer</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Content</td><td>Any markup you slot in</td><td>Circles, sprites, text</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Draggable</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Practical ceiling</td><td>~200&ndash;500 before a page feels slow</td><td>Tens of thousands</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hover / select</td><td>CSS</td><td><code>feature-state</code> expressions</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Nothing auto-switches at a threshold.</strong> Crossing it would silently delete the
      ability to put a badge in a marker &mdash; the only reason to use this component. Past the
      ceiling the answer is a GL layer, chosen by you.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="relocation" title="Why the element you write stays put">
    <p>
      MapLibre takes ownership of whatever element it is given for a marker and
      <strong>appends it into its own container</strong>, inside <code>esa-map</code>&rsquo;s shadow
      root. The obvious implementation hands it the custom element itself &mdash; and that was the
      first version here, until it was measured:
      <code>document.getElementById('my-pin')</code> returned <strong>null</strong>, because id
      lookups do not cross a shadow boundary. Every marker on the page vanished from
      <code>document.querySelectorAll</code>, silently, while rendering perfectly.
    </p>
    <p>
      A framework consumer holding a ref would never notice. A spoke wiring a plain Astro page with
      <code>getElementById</code> &mdash; which is how most of this kit is consumed &mdash; would be
      stuck. So the engine is given an inner <code>div</code>, this element stays in the light DOM
      as the addressable handle, and your slotted content is <strong>moved once</strong> into that
      div. Clicks are forwarded back, so a listener on
      <code>&lt;esa-map-marker&gt;</code> means what it looks like it means.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">
      Two consequences worth knowing. Content is adopted <strong>once</strong>, at mount &mdash; the
      element is empty afterwards, so re-render the marker rather than mutating its children. And
      because the content div lives one shadow tree up, it is styled from <code>esa-map</code>, not
      from here; anything that must travel with the marker travels as a custom property, which
      inherits across the boundary.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="api" title="API">
    <ApiTable slug="esa-map-marker" describe={describe} events={events} />
    <p class="meta">
      <strong>There is no click event.</strong> The marker is real DOM, so a native
      <code>click</code> listener already works and already tells you which marker was hit. Adding
      a synthetic one would mean a composed <code>CustomEvent</code> named <code>click</code>,
      indistinguishable from a real one &mdash; whose <code>detail</code> is the click
      <em>count</em>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</ComponentDoc>

<style>
  .api-table table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .api-table th, .api-table td { text-align: left; padding: var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem) var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-subtle, #efefef); vertical-align: top; }
  .meta { color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464); max-width: 72ch; }
</style>
